/**
\addtogroup optimizers_multistart

This page documents the multistart wrapper \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_optimizer "multistart_optimizer" (`multistart_optimizer<Inner>`) in namespace `laplace::optimizers`, defined in `<laplace/optimizers/multistart.hpp>`. It is a header-only template that runs on the host only; nothing is marked `LAPLACE_HD` and no library is linked. It executes any inner optimizer `Inner` sequentially from several starting points and keeps the best run, using the objectives and report documented on \ref optimizers "the optimizers page" and any inner method such as BFGS or L-BFGS on \ref optimizers_quasi_newton "the quasi-Newton page". Because it models \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_for "optimizer_for" for every objective accepted by `Inner`, a run is `minimize(multistart_optimizer<bfgs_optimizer<>>{}, f, x0)`. On the four-dimensional Styblinski-Tang function started in the basin of its worst minimum, BFGS from \f$40\f$ starts finds all \f$5\f$ distinct optimum values including the global minimum in \f$0.49\f$ ms.

## Starting points

The wrapper takes its starting points either from caller-supplied vectors or from deterministic Latin hypercube sampling around \f$x_0\f$ (McKay, Beckman, and Conover, 1979). When `starts` is not empty, its points are used as given in that order and `x0` is ignored. Otherwise `generated_starts(x0)` returns \f$x_0\f$ followed by \f$m\f$ Latin-hypercube points, where \f$m\f$ equals `start_count` minus \f$1\f$ (and `start_count` equal to \f$1\f$ gives \f$x_0\f$ alone). On each coordinate \f$j\f$, the interval \f$[x_{0,j} - h_j,\, x_{0,j} + h_j]\f$ with half-width \f$h_j = 3 \sigma \max(1, |x_{0,j}|)\f$ and \f$\sigma\f$ equal to `spread` is cut into \f$m\f$ equal strata, each used once in a shuffled order with a uniform offset inside the stratum; the exact formula is given under \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_optimizer "multistart_optimizer". A 64-bit linear congruential generator seeded with `seed` produces identical starts across platforms and compilers. Because the points live in the free parametrization of the objective, no start can fall outside the domain of a constrained parameter when the objective maps the constraints away.

## Run selection

Starts are executed one after the other and compared in execution order so that the wrapper keeps the best run rather than the last. A run whose objective value is not finite is never kept while a finite run exists; if every run produces a non-finite value, the first run is reported. A converged run, for which \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_succeeded "optimizer_succeeded()" returns `true`, always beats a non-converged run regardless of their objective values. Between two runs of the same convergence kind, a later run replaces the kept run only when its objective value is lower by more than `distinct_tolerance`.

## Report and counts

Calling `run(objective, x0)`, or \ref laplace::optimizers::minimize "minimize()" on a `multistart_optimizer`, returns the kept run's \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_report "optimizer_report" with the iterations, the evaluation counts and the time summed over all starts. Calling `run_multistart(objective, x0)` returns a \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_report "multistart_report", which adds every start's own report and counts of the starts, of the converged starts, of the distinct optima and of the starts that found the kept value. A count of distinct optima above \f$1\f$ shows that the objective has several local minima; the rule that counts them is given in the entry of `multistart_report`.

## Settings

The settings are public members of \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_optimizer "multistart_optimizer", whose entry gives each default and its effect: the inner optimizer, the number of starts (10), the caller's starts, the spread (1), the seed (1) and `distinct_tolerance` (\f$10^{-5}\f$). The tolerance should not be tighter than the accuracy of the inner optimizer's stopping rule, since runs that reach the same minimum differ in value by about that accuracy.

## Measurements and limits

Measured with gcc on one thread using `bfgs_optimizer` inside on the Styblinski-Tang function
\f[ f(x) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^n \left(x_i^4 - 16 x_i^2 + 5 x_i\right) \f]
from \f$x_0 = (2.5, \dots, 2.5)\f$, which lies in the basin of the worst of its \f$2^n\f$ local minima (taking \f$n + 1\f$ distinct optimum values), a single start remains at the worst minimum while multiple starts find the global minimum.

| Dimension \f$n\f$ | Starts | Converged | Distinct optima | Global found | Evaluations of \f$f\f$ and \f$g\f$ | Time (ms) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | no | 6 | 0.010 |
| 2 | 10 | 10 | 3 | yes | 130 | 0.086 |
| 2 | 40 | 40 | 3 | yes | 533 | 0.33 |
| 4 | 10 | 10 | 3 | no | 193 | 0.12 |
| 4 | 40 | 40 | 5 | yes | 805 | 0.49 |

The tests check the selection rule on scripted runs (converged against non-converged in both orders, the tolerance, non-finite values, and keeping the best rather than the last), the optimum counts on a scripted list, the generated starts (\f$x_0\f$ first, each stratum visited once on every coordinate, determinism for the same seed and distinct points for another seed), caller-given starts, and the global minimum of Styblinski-Tang for \f$n = 2\f$ and \f$n = 4\f$ with \f$40\f$ starts within \f$10^{-5}\f$ in value. See `docs/numerics.md` for details of the measurements.

The runtime cost is the sum of the inner runs plus negligible start generation, and memory holds the starts and one report per start, each \f$O(n)\f$ in the parameter dimension. Starts run sequentially on one thread; generated starts cover only the box around `x0`, so a minimum outside it is found only if an inner run walks there; selection and counts compare values, so two minima within `distinct_tolerance` count as one optimum; and whether the kept run is a minimum is as good as the inner optimizer's own verdict.

## Example

The example minimizes the one-dimensional function \f$f(x) = x^4 - 3 x^2 + x\f$ from \f$x_0 = 1.5\f$ using \f$10\f$ starts with BFGS and returns the number of distinct optima found, after `Kokkos::initialize`.

```cpp
#include <laplace/optimizers/bfgs.hpp>
#include <laplace/optimizers/multistart.hpp>

struct double_well_objective {
  double value(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x) const {
    const double v = x(0);
    return v * v * v * v - 3.0 * v * v + v;
  }
  double value_gradient(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x,
                        const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector& g) const {
    const double v = x(0);
    g(0) = 4.0 * v * v * v - 6.0 * v + 1.0;
    return v * v * v * v - 3.0 * v * v + v;
  }
};

int double_well_example() {
  laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector x0("x0", 1);
  x0(0) = 1.5;
  double_well_objective f;
  laplace::optimizers::multistart_optimizer<laplace::optimizers::bfgs_optimizer<>> ms;
  ms.start_count = 10;
  const laplace::optimizers::multistart_report r = ms.run_multistart(f, x0);
  return r.distinct_optimum_count;
}
```

## Module types

In namespace `laplace::optimizers`:
- \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_report "multistart_report"
- \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_optimizer "multistart_optimizer"

## References

- M. D. McKay, R. J. Beckman and W. J. Conover (1979), Comparison of three methods for selecting values of input variables in the analysis of output from a computer code, Technometrics, 21(2), 239–245. https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1979.10489755

*/
